# Endpoints the runtime may reach directly, one `hostname port` pair per line.
#
# This file is for protocols the HTTP proxy cannot carry. Postgres, MySQL,
# Redis, a broker. Anything speaking HTTP belongs in allowlist.txt instead,
# where the rule is a name that stays true rather than an address that drifts.
#
# The known weak point of the design, stated plainly: these become IP rules, and
# a managed endpoint's addresses are usually not contractually stable. Two
# things are built for that — set entries expire unless re-confirmed, and the
# resolver unit exits non-zero when a name stops resolving, so a stale rule
# surfaces as a failed unit rather than as an agent that mysteriously loses its
# database days later.
#
# Each line opens exactly that port on the addresses that name resolves to.

# ep-example-12345.us-east-1.aws.neon.tech 5432
# cache.internal.example 6379
